# DURABLE REPRO — the TUPLE-FIELD GET-CHAIN, BOTH FACES.
#
# This is the class that survives EVERY `&`-of-projection fix, and it is the
# reason the struct get-chain fallback was DESCOPED from Track C: closing the
# struct chain does NOT close this one, so the "one fallback retires the class"
# argument is false.
#
# The root is an asymmetry ONE LEVEL UP from the resolvers: a get-chain
# fallback wires into the `FieldAccess` arm only. `TupleFieldAccess` gets
# nothing, and `try_resolve_tuple_field_place` has no method-chain arm either.
# ⚠ NOT a 2-line mirror: `resolve_ptr_field_place` (stmts/assigns.rs:838) is
# struct-field-specific (it takes a `field_name: &str`), so this needs a
# tuple-index analogue or a genuine chain arm.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg, C and LLVM identical):
#   `&` face      `bumpi(&vt.get(0).unwrap().0)`  -> prints 10   (want 11)
#   assign face   `wt.get(0).unwrap().0 = 42`     -> prints 10   (want 42)
# `ggdef` is CORRECT on BOTH faces (11 and 42) — it adjudicates AGAINST us,
# which makes this a textbook Core #8 (a silent wrong-output both backends
# agree on) rather than a mere gap.
#
# INTENDED (what this fixture asserts): 11 then 42.
#
# The seeds are COMPUTED, not literals, so const-folding cannot elide the
# store on the real path (Core #11).
#
# Sibling faces that ALREADY write through, for contrast — a fix must not
# regress them: `v[i].a = x` (struct field of an element) and the struct
# get-chain `b.get(0).unwrap().fd = 11`.

int seed():
    return 10

void bumpi(int &x):
    x = x + 1

void main():
    Vector[(int, int)] vt = [(seed(), 5)]
    bumpi(&vt.get(0).unwrap().0)
    print(vt.get(0).unwrap().0)

    Vector[(int, int)] wt = [(seed(), 5)]
    wt.get(0).unwrap().0 = 42
    print(wt.get(0).unwrap().0)
